-- title:
--   The LX diameter equals the maximum of all pairwise word distances
-- statement:
--   The LX diameter equals the maximum of all pairwise word distances.
--
--   Let $n>0$ and write $I_n=\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$, represented by $0,\ldots,n-1$. The symbol $\operatorname{rep}$ takes a residue to its nonnegative representative and leaves ordinary integers unchanged. Residue sums and differences are cyclic; inequalities use representatives. $\mathfrak S(I_n)$ is the permutation group. $\mathcal P_{\rm fin}(X)$ denotes finite subsets and a subscript $\#$ denotes cardinality. Function symbols are written in curried notation. $\operatorname{Diam}(n)$ is the maximum shortest word length over permutations, using forward rotation and adjacent exchange. $\operatorname{dist}(a,b)$ is the shortest length of a word carrying $a$ to $b$ by right multiplication. All maxima range over the indicated finite permutation groups.
--
--   The precise quantified assertion, with all its hypotheses, is
--
--   $$
--   \forall  (n : \mathbb N_{>0}) , \operatorname{Diam} n = \max \lambda a \mapsto \max \lambda b \mapsto \operatorname{dist} a b
--   $$
--
--   This result is used to constrain the remaining phases or to construct a sorting word with the stated cost.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/snow-ghost/glushkov-lx-lean/blob/8cddcd222b591673dc63475d9d45b9ed5c6eb92c/LX/SharpDiameterAll.lean#L39-L53

